A||||7||Homicidal||Will me amplifier overheat if placed under the seat? ||||1||
Z||000000||Homicidal||04-12-2000||06:00 PM||||I can't really mount it behind the seat since the cd changer is there. It's a tight squeeze under the seat, I hope it<BR>                          doesn't damage the amp||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||04-12-2000||07:04 PM||||it will run hot. there's zilch for space or air movement down there. Depending on just how hot it gets it could melt the seat foam or start a fire.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Homicidal||04-12-2000||07:11 PM||||Well, the amp is  150W X 2 channels, and it's not a high quality amp, so do you really think it can get that hot?<P>What about some sort of fan?||||reg||1
Z||000003||N_Man||04-12-2000||07:58 PM||||I have my amp mounted in the trunk area behind the engine.  It works great and looks good.  You'll save on the power wire and use more speaker wire.  Just be sure to use plastic wire shields through the engine compartment.||||reg||1
Z||000004||mbramble||04-13-2000||07:35 AM||||I took mine to the other end of the car.  My son and I pondered long and hard about where to mount the 100x4 amp.  Finally he said 'why don't you mount it on the spare tire?'.  Well.....  What we ended up doing was turning the spare tire over so that you have the deep side pointing up, a very large 'bowlfull' of space!  I cut out a wood block, about the size of the footprint of the amp, mounted the amp to it and then mounted the block to the spare tire using bolts thru the wood block, thru the lug holes and wingnuts so it can be easlily removed.  Then I used large multipin removeable connectors for each side of the amps connections (in- and output sides).  To use the spare tire, I remove two connectors, pull the spare tire from the car and remove 3 wingnuts to remove the amp from the spare.  Worked out pretty neat.  Ran all the wires thru a hole in the frame I found around the steering column that had a plug over it.  <P>The only negative thing I can think of, is that it probably kills any chances of moving the battery to the front.  Though I've never seen the details of that mod, I would guess that the deep side of the spare tire turned down provides some of the clearance for the battery??  We could have mounted it with the shallow side up but had very little clearance between the amp and the spare tire hold-down 'strap'.  Differing physical dimensions of amps may make this mounting easier, or maybe not practical.||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||04-13-2000||02:28 PM||||correct, you can't do that and have the battery up under the tire.||||reg||1
Z||000006||fierosound||04-14-2000||01:30 AM||||I also wanted to keep the amp away from the engine heat in the trunk, and mounted it up front.  I mounted in a box over the spare tire that swings up for spare tire removal.  Pics on my website at   <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierosound" TARGET=_blank>www.geocities.com/fierosound</A> ||||reg||10
Z||000007||Racer 1||04-14-2000||01:36 AM||||Just mount it behind the seat. THe front tire mounting idea is bad unless you have good weather seals. The trunk is also bad it gets hot in there. Under the seat is the worst. you can mount a amp right above the passengers feet to the plastic duct housing.||||reg||5
